# Translations

## One change, one commit

- English changes first.
  The Russian and the Chinese page receive the same change in the same commit,
  and a translation does not lag on purpose.
- The structure is identical:
  the same headings, tables, code blocks and list items, in the same order.
  `bun run check:docs` compares their counts.
- A new term goes into `docs/glossary.md` in all three languages before it is used.

## Never translated

- Code identifiers in backticks, file names, paths, commands, URLs and script names,
  numbers inside code, shot ids, the labels that the film draws on screen,
  and the preset names `draft`, `high` and `master`.
- Names of researchers stay in Latin letters.
  A citation takes the local form: «Held и др., 2010», «Held 等，2010».

## Translated

- Prose, headings, table cells, the alt text of images,
  comments inside illustrative code blocks, and the node labels of diagrams, with the syntax kept.
- The language switchers link the three versions of a page to each other:
  `README.md`, `README.ru.md`, `README.zh.md`, and `docs/<lang>/README.md`.
  Links between pages stay relative inside the language folder.
- Every number, caveat and source keeps its exact meaning.
  Where the English marks something as craft and not as research, the translation keeps the mark.

## Russian

- A decimal comma in prose (2,1 с; 1,8–2,6 с) and a decimal point inside code; «ёлочки» for quotes.
- Natural word order, not a calque of the English.
- No «не X, а Y» and no «не просто X, а Y».

## Chinese

- Full-width punctuation in Chinese sentences,
  and a half-width space between Chinese characters and Latin words or numbers:
  2.5D 动态 UI, RTX 3060 Ti 上约 20 帧每秒.
- Punctuation stands outside bold: `**每一帧都是时间的函数**。`, and a label is `**我们的用法**：`.
  GitHub does not close a bold span when a colon inside it is followed by a Chinese character.
- Lines break only after full-width punctuation — [line-breaks](line-breaks.mdc).
- No 不是……而是……, and no ……而不是…….
